Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action, 1843 London. Gaslight, fog, candle-flame.

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

Fog presses at the window. A signboard outside reads SCROOGE AND MARLEY. Inside, EBENEZER SCROOGE — gaunt, pointed nose, thin blue lips, frost-white eyebrows, black skullcap, threadbare black coat — hunches over a ledger by one mean candle. Beyond a partition, in a cell of a room, BOB CRATCHIT — small, thin, white comforter wound three times round his throat, no great-coat — copies letters over a fire of a single coal.

(SOUND: church bell strikes three in the fog; wheezing footsteps in the court outside)

NARRATOR
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

Scrooge dips his pen. Cratchit breathes on his fingers, then tries to warm them at his candle. He fails.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Oh! But he was a tight-fisted hand at the grindstone, Scrooge! a squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching, covetous, old sinner! Hard and sharp as flint. Secret, and self-contained, and solitary as an oyster.

The door bursts open. FRED — Scrooge's nephew, ruddy young man, glowing cheeks, snow on his shoulders, sprig of holly in his lapel — comes in all in a glow.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug. If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with "Merry Christmas" on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart. He should!

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time; the only time I know of, in the long calendar of the year, when men and women seem by one consent to open their shut-up hearts freely. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

Cratchit applauds — then, appalled at himself, pokes the fire and extinguishes his last frail spark for ever.

(SOUND: a lone coal collapses into ash)

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I am sorry, with all my heart, to find you so resolute. But I have made the trial in homage to Christmas, and I'll keep my Christmas humour to the last. So A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred stops at the outer door and shakes Cratchit's hand warmly. He goes. In his place enters MR. HOPKINS — portly, hat in hand, books and papers under his arm — who bows.

MR. HOPKINS
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

Mr. Hopkins offers his credentials. Scrooge frowns, shakes his head, hands them back.

MR. HOPKINS
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing.

MR. HOPKINS
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. Are there no prisons?

MR. HOPKINS
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

MR. HOPKINS
They are. Still, I wish I could say they were not. Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. It's not my business. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

Mr. Hopkins withdraws. Scrooge resumes his labours, in a more facetious temper than usual. Cratchit snuffs his candle out and puts on his hat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Cratchit promises with a bow, and is gone. Scrooge buttons his great-coat to the chin and blows out the candle.

(SOUND: the door bolt shoots home; a boy's voice singing a carol outside, cut off by the wind)

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - COURTYARD - NIGHT

A black gateway up a dark yard. Fog and frost hang about it. Scrooge gropes along the wall, key in hand, and stops at the door — a very large brass knocker on it.

(SOUND: dripping water, a distant chattering bell in the clouds)

The knocker is not a knocker. It is MARLEY'S FACE — livid, dismal-lit, ghostly spectacles turned up on its ghostly forehead, hair stirred as if by hot breath, eyes wide open and perfectly motionless.

(MUSIC: low sustained drone under a thin metallic shiver)

Scrooge stares. The face is a knocker again. He puts his hand upon the key, turns it sturdily.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

He looks behind the door — nothing but screws and nuts — and slams it.

(SOUND: the bang resounds through the house like thunder, echoing in the cellars below)

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

A gloomy room. Dutch tiles round an old fireplace. A very low fire; a saucepan of gruel on the hob. Scrooge, now in dressing-gown, slippers and nightcap, double-locks the door, then sits close to the grate with his basin.

(SOUND: spoon against basin; the fire ticks)

SCROOGE
Humbug!

He throws his head back in the chair. His glance rests on a disused bell high on the wall. It begins, very softly, to swing.

(SOUND: one bell rings — then every bell in the house rings out loudly together)

The bells cease. Deep below, a clanking begins: a heavy chain dragged over casks.

(SOUND: the cellar door flies open with a boom; chains coming up the stairs)

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

His colour changes. Through the heavy locked door passes JACOB MARLEY'S GHOST — the same face, pigtail, waistcoat, tights and boots, a folded kerchief bound about his head and chin; his body transparent; a long chain clasped about his middle, made of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds and heavy purses wrought in steel. The dying flame leaps up and falls again.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then? You're particular, for a shade.

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

Marley sits down opposite him, quite at home. His hair, skirts and tassels stir as by hot vapour from an oven.

MARLEY
You don't believe in me.

SCROOGE
I don't. A slight disorder of the stomach makes the senses cheats. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

Marley raises a frightful cry and shakes his chain. He unwinds the bandage from his head — and his lower jaw drops down upon his breast. Scrooge falls upon his knees and clasps his hands before his face.

(SOUND: the chain crashes on the boards; a long unearthly wail)

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
Man of the worldly mind! It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death. It is doomed to wander through the world — oh, woe is me! — and witness what it cannot share, but might have shared on earth, and turned to happiness!

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard; I girded it on of my own free will, and of my own free will I wore it. Is its pattern strange to you?

Scrooge glances about the floor, expecting iron cable. He sees nothing. He trembles more and more.

MARLEY
Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it, since. It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
Jacob! Old Jacob Marley, speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY
I have none to give. But you were always a good man of business, were you not? Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business. The dealings of my trade were but a drop of water in the comprehensive ocean of my business!

He holds the chain out at arm's length and flings it heavily on the ground.

MARLEY
I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I — I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Without their visits you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One. The second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

Marley binds the wrapper round his head. The window raises itself, step by step, as he walks backward toward it. He holds up a warning hand.

(SOUND: confused wailings in the air — lamentation, self-accusatory, sorrowful)

Marley floats out upon the bleak night. Scrooge crowds to the window. The air is filled with phantoms in chains, wandering, moaning — one old ghost in a white waistcoat with a monstrous iron safe chained to its ankle, crying piteously at a wretched woman with an infant on a door-step below.

(MUSIC: distant choir of moaning voices thinning into silence)

Scrooge closes the window. He tries the door: double-locked, bolts undisturbed.

SCROOGE
Hum—

He cannot finish it. He goes straight to bed without undressing, and sleeps upon the instant.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - BEDROOM - NIGHT

Bed-curtains, dark. Scrooge's eyes are open. Chimes count the quarters.

(SOUND: ding, dong — ding, dong — ding, dong — ding, dong)

SCROOGE
A quarter past. Half-past. A quarter to it. The hour itself, and nothing else!

(SOUND: a deep, dull, hollow, melancholy ONE)

Light floods the room. A hand draws the curtains of his bed aside. Before him stands THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST — like a child, yet like an old man diminished; hair white as with age, no wrinkle in the face; bare arms and feet; a tunic of the purest white, a lustrous belt; a branch of fresh green holly in its hand, its dress trimmed with summer flowers; from the crown of its head springs a bright clear jet of light. Under its arm it carries a great extinguisher cap.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

Scrooge looks at the extinguisher cap and reaches for it. The Spirit's light flares.

CHRISTMAS PAST
Would you so soon put out, with worldly hands, the light I give? Rise! and walk with me!

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

The Spirit lays its hand upon his heart.

CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

They move toward the wall — and pass through it.

(MUSIC: soft high strings swelling as the room dissolves)

EXT. SCHOOLHOUSE LANE - DAY

A clear, cold winter day. Snow on the ground. An open country road with fields either hand; beyond, a market-town with its bridge, its church, its winding river. Scrooge stands in dressing-gown and slippers beside the Spirit, hands clasped.

(SOUND: crisp snow underfoot; boys shouting far off; jingling harness)

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

Shaggy ponies trot past with boys upon their backs, calling merrily to other boys in carts. Scrooge names them under his breath, his cold eye glistening.

CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us.

The boys part at the cross-roads, calling Merry Christmas. Scrooge's lip trembles.

CHRISTMAS PAST
Your lip is trembling. And what is that upon your cheek?

SCROOGE
A pimple. Lead me where you will.

CHRISTMAS PAST
The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

Scrooge sobs. Ahead, a mansion of dull red brick with a weathercock cupola, its windows broken, its gates decayed.

INT. SCHOOLROOM - DAY

A long, bare, melancholy room of plain deal forms and desks. At one desk, YOUNG EBENEZER — a thin boy of ten in a patched jacket, dark hair, the same sharp nose — reads alone by a feeble fire. Scrooge sits down upon a form and weeps.

(SOUND: mice scuffling behind the panelling; a drip from a half-thawed spout)

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

The door opens. FAN — a small girl, six or seven, dark ringlets, blue travelling cloak too big for her — comes darting in and throws her arms about the boy's neck.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! To bring you home, home, home!

YOUNG EBENEZER
Home, little Fan?

FAN
Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever. Father is so much kinder than he used to be, that home's like Heaven! And you're to be a man, and are never to come back here; but first, we're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

YOUNG EBENEZER
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

She laughs, stands on tiptoe to embrace him, and drags him to the door.

(SOUND: a chaise wheel on gravel; a coachman's whip)

CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature, whom a breath might have withered. But she had a large heart!

SCROOGE
So she had. You're right. I will not gainsay it, Spirit. God forbid!

CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Scrooge is uneasy.

SCROOGE
Yes.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

A city warehouse, lamps trimmed, shutters going up. Behind a very high desk sits OLD FEZZIWIG — stout, Welsh wig, capacious waistcoat, laughing all over himself. Scrooge and the Spirit stand unseen.

(SOUND: shutters banging into place; a fiddle tuning)

SCROOGE
Why, it's old Fezziwig! Bless his heart; it's Fezziwig alive again!

Fezziwig looks up at the clock: seven. He rubs his hands.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick!

EBENEZER THE APPRENTICE — a lean young man, brown hair, ink on his cuffs, eyes not yet hard — comes briskly in with DICK WILKINS.

SCROOGE
Dick Wilkins, to be sure! He was very much attached to me, was Dick. Poor Dick! Dear, dear!

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, my boys! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up, before a man can say Jack Robinson!

They charge the shutters into place and come back panting like race-horses. Fezziwig skips down from the high desk.

FEZZIWIG
Hilli-ho! Clear away, my lads, and let's have lots of room here! Hilli-ho, Dick! Chirrup, Ebenezer!

Every movable is packed off; the floor swept and watered, fuel heaped on the fire. In comes a fiddler, and MRS. FEZZIWIG — one vast substantial smile — and the three Miss Fezziwigs, the housemaid and the baker, the cook and the milkman.

(MUSIC: fiddle strikes up "Sir Roger de Coverley," feet drumming the boards)

Twenty couple at once — down the middle and up again. Fezziwig and Mrs. Fezziwig take top couple. He cuts so deftly he seems to wink with his legs, and comes upon his feet without a stagger. Scrooge, forgetting himself, claps and laughs aloud.

(SOUND: clock strikes eleven; laughter and Merry Christmases dying away at the door)

The Fezziwigs shake hands with every person as they go. The light on the Spirit's head burns very clear.

CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps. Is that so much that he deserves this praise?

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gives, is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

He feels the Spirit's glance and stops.

CHRISTMAS PAST
Something, I think?

SCROOGE
No. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

The apprentice turns down the lamps. Darkness swallows the warehouse.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - NIGHT

A modest parlour. Firelight. EBENEZER IN HIS PRIME — hair still brown, face lined with care, an eager greedy restlessness in the eye — sits beside BELLE, a fair young girl in a mourning-dress, tears sparkling in her eyes. Scrooge and the Spirit watch from the shadow.

(SOUND: a clock ticking; coals settling)

BELLE
It matters little. To you, very little. Another idol has displaced me.

EBENEZER IN HIS PRIME
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. You fear the world too much. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Have I not?

EBENEZER IN HIS PRIME
What then? Even if I have grown so much wiser, what then? I am not changed towards you.

She shakes her head.

BELLE
Our contract is an old one. It was made when we were both poor and content to be so. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

EBENEZER IN HIS PRIME
I was a boy.

BELLE
Your own feeling tells you that you were not what you are. I am. That which promised happiness when we were one in heart, is fraught with misery now that we are two. It is enough that I have thought of it, and can release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

She turns her head away.

BELLE
A very, very brief time, and you will dismiss the recollection of it, gladly, as an unprofitable dream, from which it happened well that you awoke. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

She leaves him. He does not follow.

(MUSIC: a simple air on a harp, one thin unresolved phrase)

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

SCROOGE
No more! I don't wish to see it. Show me no more!

The Ghost pinions him in both arms. The parlour fills with light and noise — BELLE, now a comely matron, laughing among children who conduct themselves like forty; the master of the house comes in laden with Christmas parcels and is stormed by the children.

(SOUND: children shrieking with laughter; brown paper tearing)

Scrooge's sight grows dim. The husband draws Belle and their daughter down beside him at his own fireside.

BELLE'S HUSBAND
Belle, I saw an old friend of yours this afternoon. Mr. Scrooge it was. I passed his office window; and as it was not shut up, and he had a candle inside, I could scarcely help seeing him. His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear; and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Remove me from this place. I cannot bear it!

CHRISTMAS PAST
I told you these were shadows of the things that have been. That they are what they are, do not blame me!

SCROOGE
Leave me! Take me back. Haunt me no longer!

He wrestles with the Spirit, seizes the extinguisher-cap, and presses it down upon its head with all his force. The Spirit drops beneath it — but the light streams out from under it in an unbroken flood upon the ground.

(SOUND: a long exhale of wind; the light hums)

Scrooge reels, overcome, into his own bedroom, and sinks into a heavy sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

The room has undergone a surprising transformation. Walls and ceiling hung with living green — holly, mistletoe, ivy — bright berries glistening; a mighty blaze roaring up the chimney. Heaped on the floor to form a kind of throne: turkeys, geese, brawn, great joints of meat, wreaths of sausages, mince-pies, plum-puddings, barrels of oysters, red-hot chestnuts, cherry-cheeked apples, juicy oranges, twelfth-cakes and seething bowls of punch steaming the chamber dim. Upon this couch sits THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT — a jolly Giant in one simple green robe bordered with white fur, breast bare, feet bare, dark brown curls long and free, a holly wreath set with shining icicles on his head, an empty rusted scabbard at his middle, a glowing torch shaped like Plenty's horn in his hand.

(MUSIC: warm brass and bells swelling as the door opens)

Scrooge peeps round the door, hangs his head.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never. Have you had many brothers, Spirit?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
More than eighteen hundred.

SCROOGE
A tremendous family to provide for!

The Giant rises. He fills the room.

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. I went forth last night on compulsion, and I learnt a lesson which is working now. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge takes hold and holds it fast. Green, fruit, fire and room vanish instantly.

(SOUND: a rush of wind; then church bells and street noise)

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Snow scraped from pavements and shovelled off housetops, plumping down into the road. Boys pelting a facetious snowball. Poulterers' and fruiterers' shops radiant — baskets of chestnuts, Spanish onions, pyramids of pears and apples, oranges and lemons. Scrooge and the Giant stand invisible in a baker's doorway as poor revellers carry their dinners past.

(SOUND: shovels on stone, church bells calling, a shopkeeper's twine snapping)

The Spirit lifts the covers as the bearers pass and sprinkles incense on their dinners from his torch. Two dinner-carriers jostle and snarl; he sheds a few drops of water on them, and their good humour is restored directly.

SCROOGE
Is there a peculiar flavour in what you sprinkle from your torch?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There is. My own.

SCROOGE
Would it apply to any kind of dinner on this day?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

SCROOGE
Why to a poor one most?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Because it needs it most.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - PARLOUR - CHRISTMAS DAY

A four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT — poorly dressed in a twice-turned gown, brave in cheap ribbons — lays the cloth with BELINDA. PETER, drowning in his father's monstrous shirt collar, plunges a fork into the potatoes. Two smaller Cratchits come tearing in. The Spirit stands on the threshold and blesses the dwelling with a sprinkling of his torch.

(SOUND: potatoes knocking at the saucepan-lid; children shrieking)

MRS. CRATCHIT
What has ever got your precious father then? And your brother, Tiny Tim!

MARTHA — the eldest, thin, in a milliner's apprentice shawl — appears in the doorway.

MARTHA
Here's Martha, mother! We'd a deal of work to finish up last night.

TWO SMALLER CRATCHITS
There's father coming. Hide, Martha, hide!

Martha hides. In comes BOB CRATCHIT — the same white comforter, threadbare clothes darned and brushed — with TINY TIM upon his shoulder: a small boy, pale, bright-eyed, a little crutch in his hand, his limbs supported by an iron frame.

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Not coming.

BOB CRATCHIT
Not coming! Not coming upon Christmas Day!

Martha runs out of hiding into his arms. The young Cratchits bear Tim off to the wash-house to hear the pudding singing in the copper.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day, who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

His voice is tremulous. He turns up his cuffs and stirs gin and lemons in a jug.

BOB CRATCHIT
He is growing strong and hearty.

The goose comes in high procession. Mrs. Cratchit looks slowly along the carving-knife and plunges it in; the long-expected gush of stuffing issues forth.

(SOUND: one murmur of delight round the board; Tim beating the table with his knife-handle)

TINY TIM
Hurrah!

Mrs. Cratchit leaves the room alone, too nervous to bear witnesses — and returns flushed but smiling proudly with the pudding, like a speckled cannon-ball, blazing in brandy, holly stuck into the top.

(SOUND: brandy catching with a soft whoosh; applause)

BOB CRATCHIT
I regard it as the greatest success achieved by Mrs. Cratchit since our marriage.

The cloth cleared, the hearth swept, chestnuts on the fire. Two tumblers and a custard-cup without a handle. Bob serves out the hot stuff with beaming looks.

(SOUND: chestnuts sputtering and cracking)

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

ALL THE CRATCHITS
God bless us!

Tiny Tim sits very close to his father's side upon his little stool. Bob holds his withered little hand.

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Scrooge watches from the corner with an interest he has never felt before.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head to hear his own words quoted back.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered What the surplus is, and Where it is. Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die?

Scrooge bends before the rebuke, and casts his eyes upon the ground. Bob raises his cup.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon, and I hope he'd have a good appetite for it.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear — the children! Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year!

The children drink it without heartiness. Tiny Tim drinks it last of all, and doesn't care twopence for it. A dark shadow lies on the party — then lifts, and they are ten times merrier than before.

(MUSIC: Tiny Tim sings a plaintive little song about a lost child travelling in the snow)

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - NIGHT

Lamplight, dessert on the table, a fire. FRED — holly still in his lapel — holds his sides with laughter. His wife, SCROOGE'S NIECE — dimpled, bright-eyed, harp beside her chair — laughs as heartily. TOPPER and the guests roar. Scrooge and the Giant stand smiling behind her chair.

(SOUND: laughter rolling round the room; a bottle passed and glasses ringing)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

SCROOGE'S NIECE
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
He's a comical old fellow, that's the truth: and not so pleasant as he might be. However, his offences carry their own punishment, and I have nothing to say against him.

SCROOGE'S NIECE
I have no patience with him.

FRED
Oh, I have! I am sorry for him; I couldn't be angry with him if I tried. Who suffers by his ill whims! Himself, always. I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not, for I pity him. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it — I defy him — if he finds me going there, in good temper, year after year, and saying Uncle Scrooge, how are you?

Scrooge's Niece plays a simple little air upon the harp. Scrooge stands very still; his hard face softens.

(MUSIC: the same thin harp air heard in Belle's parlour, now complete)

They play at blind-man's buff. Topper, blindfold, pursues the plump sister past chairs and curtains, always knowing exactly where she is. Then forfeits, and Yes and No, Scrooge calling out his guesses aloud, unheard.

FRED
An animal, a live animal, rather a disagreeable animal, a savage animal, an animal that growled and grunted sometimes, and lived in London, and walked about the streets—

THE PLUMP SISTER
I have found it out! I know what it is, Fred!

FRED
What is it?

THE PLUMP SISTER
It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-o-oge!

Laughter. Fred lifts a glass of mulled wine.

FRED
He has given us plenty of merriment, I am sure, and it would be ungrateful not to drink his health. Uncle Scrooge! A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! He wouldn't take it from me, but may he have it, nevertheless. Uncle Scrooge!

ALL THE GUESTS
Uncle Scrooge!

Scrooge, gay and light of heart, raises an unseen hand to pledge them — and the whole scene passes off in the breath of the last word.

EXT. OPEN PLACE OUTSIDE THE CITY - NIGHT

A bare open place under a black sky. Scrooge stares at the Giant: his dark curls are grey now, his face older.

(SOUND: chimes ringing three quarters past eleven; a rising cold wind)

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends to-night. To-night at midnight. Hark! The time is drawing near.

SCROOGE
Forgive me if I am not justified in what I ask, but I see something strange, and not belonging to yourself, protruding from your skirts. Is it a foot or a claw?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
It might be a claw, for the flesh there is upon it. Look here.

From the foldings of his robe he brings two children — a boy and girl; yellow, meagre, ragged, wolfish; prostrate in their humility. They cling to the outside of his garment.

(MUSIC: the warm brass curdles into a low, cold string tremolo)

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! look here. Look, look, down here! This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

(SOUND: the bell strikes twelve, stroke on stroke)

The Giant is gone. As the last stroke ceases to vibrate, Scrooge lifts his eyes: a solemn Phantom comes toward him like a mist along the ground — THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME, shrouded in a deep black garment that conceals head, face and form, leaving nothing visible save one outstretched hand.

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come?

The Spirit answers not, but points onward.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But as I know your purpose is to do me good, and as I hope to live to be another man from what I was, I am prepared to bear you company. Will you not speak to me?

No reply. The hand is pointed straight before them.

SCROOGE
Lead on! The night is waning fast, and it is precious time to me, I know. Lead on, Spirit!

EXT. 'CHANGE - DAY

Merchants hurry up and down, chinking money in their pockets, trifling with their great gold seals. The Phantom stops beside a knot of business men: MR. TUPPER, vast-chinned, and MR. GRUB, red-faced, with a great snuff-box.

(SOUND: coins chinking; snuff sniffed; a hard laugh)

MR. TUPPER
No, I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

MR. GRUB
When did he die?

MR. TUPPER
Last night, I believe.

MR. GRUB
Why, what was the matter with him? I thought he'd never die.

MR. TUPPER
God knows.

MR. GRUB
What has he done with his money?

MR. TUPPER
I haven't heard. Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me. That's all I know. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral; for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it. Suppose we make up a party and volunteer?

MR. GRUB
I don't mind going if a lunch is provided. But I must be fed, if I make one.

They laugh and stroll away into other groups. Scrooge looks to the Spirit for an explanation. The hand only points on.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG SHOP - NIGHT

A low-browed, beetling shop. Heaps of rusty keys, nails, chains, scales, weights; mountains of unseemly rags. OLD JOE — grey-haired, near seventy, pipe in his teeth — sits by a charcoal stove of old bricks behind a curtain of tatters. MRS. DILBER the laundress, THE CHARWOMAN with a heavy bundle, and MR. BAGGS the undertaker's man in faded black, meet and burst into a laugh.

(SOUND: the shop door skreeks on rusty hinges; a lamp wick guttering)

THE CHARWOMAN
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! Let the laundress alone to be the second; and let the undertaker's man alone to be the third. Look here, old Joe, here's a chance!

OLD JOE
You couldn't have met in a better place. Come into the parlour.

The Charwoman throws her bundle on the floor and sits on a stool, elbows crossed on her knees, bold as brass. Scrooge watches from the shadow.

THE CHARWOMAN
Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did.

MRS. DILBER
That's true, indeed! No man more so.

THE CHARWOMAN
Who's the worse for the loss of a few things like these? Not a dead man, I suppose. If he wanted to keep 'em after he was dead, a wicked old screw, why wasn't he natural in his lifetime? If he had been, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death, instead of lying gasping out his last there, alone by himself.

MRS. DILBER
It's a judgment on him.

Mr. Baggs produces his plunder: a seal or two, a pencil-case, a pair of sleeve-buttons, a brooch of no great value. Joe chalks his sums upon the wall. Mrs. Dilber's sheets and towels and silver teaspoons follow. Joe kneels to open the Charwoman's bundle and drags out a heavy roll of dark stuff.

OLD JOE
What do you call this? Bed-curtains!

THE CHARWOMAN
Bed-curtains!

OLD JOE
You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

THE CHARWOMAN
Yes I do. Why not? Don't drop that oil upon the blankets, now.

OLD JOE
His blankets?

THE CHARWOMAN
Whose else's do you think? He isn't likely to take cold without 'em, I dare say. Ah! you may look through that shirt till your eyes ache; but you won't find a hole in it. It's the best he had. They'd have wasted it, if it hadn't been for me — putting it on him to be buried in.

She laughs. Joe tells out their gains from a flannel bag upon the ground.

(SOUND: coins spilling onto bare boards; three voices laughing together)

THE CHARWOMAN
This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

Scrooge shudders from head to foot.

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

INT. DEAD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

A bare, uncurtained bed in a very dark room. Beneath a ragged sheet lies a something covered up. A pale light from the outer air falls straight upon the bed. Plundered and bereft, unwatched, unwept, uncared for.

(SOUND: a cat tearing at the door; rats gnawing beneath the hearth-stone)

The Phantom's steady hand points to the head. The cover is so carelessly adjusted that the motion of a finger would disclose the face. Scrooge's hand rises — and stops, trembling, in the air.

(MUSIC: a single held low note, almost inaudible)

SCROOGE
Spirit, this is a fearful place. In leaving it, I shall not leave its lesson, trust me. Let us go!

The finger does not move from the head.

SCROOGE
I understand you, and I would do it, if I could. But I have not the power, Spirit. I have not the power.

Scrooge sinks to his knees beside the bed and cannot look at it.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

The Phantom spreads its dark robe before him like a wing.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - PARLOUR - EVENING

Quiet. Very quiet. The noisy little Cratchits are as still as statues in one corner. PETER — collars gone, a book open before him — has stopped reading. MRS. CRATCHIT and her daughters sew by candle-light. In the chimney-corner stands a little crutch, carefully preserved, and an empty stool.

(SOUND: a needle drawn through cloth; the candle sputters)

PETER
"And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them."

Mrs. Cratchit lays her work upon the table and puts her hand up to her face.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes. They're better now again. It makes them weak by candle-light; and I wouldn't show weak eyes to your father when he comes home, for the world. It must be near his time.

PETER
Past it rather. But I think he has walked a little slower than he used, these few last evenings, mother.

Silence again. She keeps her eyes on her work; her voice is steady, and falters only once.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with — I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed.

PETER
And so have I. Often.

MRS. CRATCHIT
But he was very light to carry, and his father loved him so, that it was no trouble: no trouble. And there is your father at the door!

Bob comes in, in his comforter. The two young Cratchits get upon his knees and lay a cheek each against his face. He looks at the work upon the table and praises it.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Sunday! You went to-day, then, Robert?

BOB CRATCHIT
Yes, my dear. I wish you could have gone. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. But you'll see it often. I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child! My little child!

He breaks down all at once. He cannot help it.

(SOUND: a chair scraping; footsteps climbing the stairs above)

He goes up to the room above, lighted cheerfully and hung with Christmas, and sits in the chair set close beside where the child lay. He composes himself, and kisses the little face. Then he comes down again quite happy, and draws to the fire.

BOB CRATCHIT
However and whenever we part from one another, I am sure we shall none of us forget poor Tiny Tim — shall we — or this first parting that there was among us?

ALL THE CRATCHITS
Never, father!

BOB CRATCHIT
And I know, my dears, that when we recollect how patient and how mild he was; although he was a little, little child; we shall not quarrel easily among ourselves, and forget poor Tiny Tim in doing it. I am very happy. I am very happy!

Scrooge watches from the doorway, unseen, his face working.

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. I know it, but I know not how. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead?

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

An iron gate. A churchyard walled in by houses; overrun by grass and weeds; choked up with too much burying. The Phantom stands among the graves and points down to One.

(SOUND: wind through dead weeds; the iron gate groaning on its hinge)

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

Still the Ghost points downward to the grave.

SCROOGE
Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

The Spirit is immovable. Scrooge creeps toward the stone, trembling, and brushes away the weeds. He reads: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

(MUSIC: a shattering low chord, then silence)

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay
